#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183996 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3155a85f-7bd8-584c-a5ae-e2e18dd60cd2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Sine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the First Quadrant"}] :question/id #uuid "c049aaef-f59a-5113-b739-20cc7a6d802c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3520" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin u = \\dfrac{3}{4}, \\qquad \\cos u =  -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}, \\qquad \\cos v = \\dfrac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, and\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(u + v\\right) = \\dfrac{a- b\\sqrt 7}{20},\n$$\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b.$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(u+v) = \\sin u \\cos v+ \\cos u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\sin{u},$ $\\cos{u},$ and $\\cos{v}.$ To find the missing value of $\\sin{v},$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 v + \\cos^2 v= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\sin^2 v = 1- \\cos^2 v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{4}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{25}-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{25-16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\sin v= \\dfrac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{4})(\\frac{4}{5})+(-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4})(\\frac{3}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{20}-\\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{12-3\\sqrt{7}}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=12, b=3,$ and $a+b = 15.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184076 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "99d7256f-6130-52b4-8188-3b1388c63f2b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Sine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the Second, Third, or Fourth Quadrants"}] :question/id #uuid "06df772e-eb43-55e8-9ede-3fc2f7ae291d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9984" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\cos u = \\dfrac{7}{25}, \\qquad \\sin v = \\dfrac{4}{5}, \\qquad \\cos v = \\dfrac {3} {5}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the fourth quadrant, find the value of $\\sin(u-v).$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(u-v) = \\sin u \\cos v- \\cos u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\cos{u}$, $\\sin{v}$, and $\\cos{v}.$ To find the missing value of $\\sin{u}$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 u + \\cos^2 u= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\sin^2 u = 1- \\cos^2 u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡u & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{7}{25})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{7}^{2}}{{25}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{49}{625} \\\\  & =\\frac{625}{625}-\\frac{49}{625} \\\\  & =\\frac{576}{625}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{576}{625}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{24}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the fourth quadrant, where sine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\sin u= -\\dfrac{24}{25}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\sin(u - v) \n\t\t\t&= \\sin u \\cos v - \\cos u \\sin v \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= \\left( -\\dfrac {24}{25} \\right)\\left(\\dfrac {3} {5}\\right)\n \t\t\t\t -  \\left(\\dfrac {7}{25}\\right) \\left( \\dfrac {4} {5}\\right) \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\n \t\t\t&= -\\dfrac {72} {125} -  \\dfrac {28} {125} \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= -\\dfrac {100} {125}\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= -\\dfrac {4} {5}.\n\t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184396 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "99d7256f-6130-52b4-8188-3b1388c63f2b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Sine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the Second, Third, or Fourth Quadrants"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d540adb0-4822-5764-8a8a-d96c8e4c2e9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186180826} {:answer/id #uuid "03888c6d-eae5-5e8c-a2b1-1b9b021a205c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186180827} {:answer/id #uuid "0995b034-c51a-560f-9e1e-befb26b7f1f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186180828} {:answer/id #uuid "34f6a551-a30b-5bdb-bc46-03ba79c9bfd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186180829} {:answer/id #uuid "44f85a2f-1097-5205-93f2-92cfdb168e55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186180830}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44f85a2f-1097-5205-93f2-92cfdb168e55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186180830} :answer-field/id #uuid "a24e2409-8ab5-5ff2-b172-35006aeb5209" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186180825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ba92bee5-a4eb-55dd-b31e-91c198d4e5df" :question/math-academy-id "q-119179" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{4},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=-\\frac{5}{6}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the second quadrant, and\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\frac{a\\sqrt{5}-b}{24},\n$$\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b.$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(-\\frac{5}{6})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{5}^{2}}{{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{25}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{36}-\\frac{25}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{36}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{36}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the second quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4})(-\\frac{5}{6})-(\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{4})(\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}}{24}-\\frac{11}{24} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}-11}{24}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=5,b=11,$ and $a+b=16.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315785 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3155a85f-7bd8-584c-a5ae-e2e18dd60cd2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Sine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the First Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e8b577d-da0e-58cb-8ac6-03f8b23d746d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315787} {:answer/id #uuid "8b908d01-a4ec-5504-b643-849aa7bbcf78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315788} {:answer/id #uuid "6997b64d-dfaa-5ecc-823d-d7cb4647e3cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315789} {:answer/id #uuid "6f1a304f-fc86-5afc-a7cf-00a9a71156e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315790} {:answer/id #uuid "32e81538-e77b-5b20-b4fe-435e0e246a55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315791}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e8b577d-da0e-58cb-8ac6-03f8b23d746d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315787} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0d2587a-ab25-5ee9-a5ac-94c90771229d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315786}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "86f6d86f-10c5-5e67-9510-1ded8a2565c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-119451" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{2}{7},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=-\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, and\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\frac{a-b\\sqrt{55}}{42},\n$$\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b.$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡u=1-{sin}^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{11})^{2}}{{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{11}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{36}-\\frac{11}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{36-11}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{36}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{5}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6})(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7})+(\\frac{5}{6})(\\frac{2}{7}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{55}}{42}+\\frac{10}{42} \\\\  & =\\frac{10-3\\sqrt{55}}{42}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=10,b=3,$ and $a+b=13.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315792 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3155a85f-7bd8-584c-a5ae-e2e18dd60cd2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Sine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the First Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58a76212-1ff3-5106-8e59-25a937646fa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{36}{85}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315794} {:answer/id #uuid "c3834233-b2e6-5c29-9cff-1b9daa09aff9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315795} {:answer/id #uuid "e57a8a22-fc31-5f63-bec9-5d7359c92af3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{84}{85}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315796} {:answer/id #uuid "f4941780-e8fd-5e5c-9c48-90e637639835" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315797} {:answer/id #uuid "b2978b18-344f-5ad5-9710-11365328d914" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315798}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58a76212-1ff3-5106-8e59-25a937646fa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{36}{85}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315794} :answer-field/id #uuid "508f49df-0149-5c45-bdbf-5dcf6cbd578e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315793}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c8ec83c2-6161-5620-8594-419c2bce3774" :question/math-academy-id "q-141357" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{8}{17},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{4}{5},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, find the value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v).$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡u=1-{sin}^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{8}{17})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{8}^{2}}{{17}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{64}{289} \\\\  & =\\frac{289}{289}-\\frac{64}{289} \\\\  & =\\frac{225}{289}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{225}{289}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{15}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{15}{17}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{8}{17})(\\frac{3}{5})-(\\frac{15}{17})(\\frac{4}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{85}-\\frac{60}{85} \\\\  & =-\\frac{36}{85}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315799 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3155a85f-7bd8-584c-a5ae-e2e18dd60cd2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Sine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the First Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7041e7e3-3187-5b0b-be81-87b568e46d3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{36}{85}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315801} {:answer/id #uuid "9661d1aa-18ab-5cd1-b988-4402c4080cf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{84}{85}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315802} {:answer/id #uuid "1e946eb2-e192-508e-8feb-dcc93a30a667" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{77}{85}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315803} {:answer/id #uuid "2f9ee248-28a8-5859-8760-419ed3fc3b4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{85}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315804} {:answer/id #uuid "31f3504e-d915-54d8-aa75-668338a7add3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{43}{85}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315805}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e946eb2-e192-508e-8feb-dcc93a30a667" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{77}{85}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315803} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c9cf168-7b8c-51ad-94bf-b408a4469ab5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9d9c039f-fd01-5ee7-bb06-59cc31c86fd0" :question/math-academy-id "q-143698" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{3}{5},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{8}{17},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=-\\frac{15}{17}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, find the value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v).$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡u=1-{sin}^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{9}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{25}-\\frac{9}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{5})(-\\frac{15}{17})-(\\frac{4}{5})(\\frac{8}{17}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{45}{85}-\\frac{32}{85} \\\\  & =-\\frac{77}{85}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315806 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "99d7256f-6130-52b4-8188-3b1388c63f2b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Sine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the Second, Third, or Fourth Quadrants"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "212303d5-8826-5186-b564-0e30d371283d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315808} {:answer/id #uuid "86f07146-1c72-5e07-99ff-e88416bd8632" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{117}{125}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315809} {:answer/id #uuid "87350162-9970-5659-adf3-18e2fb061a6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315810} {:answer/id #uuid "c630066d-ad44-5de4-9192-9c9784ed02c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{44}{125}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315811} {:answer/id #uuid "ae1312ea-a76e-5086-9cbc-80b9953f58c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{37}{125}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315812}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c630066d-ad44-5de4-9192-9c9784ed02c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{44}{125}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315811} :answer-field/id #uuid "361991d3-d38a-58ec-9686-252df8d1d544" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315807}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2ea80315-6d56-575c-ae3c-a76c0997ca5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-144398" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{7}{25},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=\\frac{3}{5},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=-\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $x$ lies in the fourth quadrant, find the value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡x=1-{sin}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡x & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1-{(-\\frac{7}{25})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(-7)^{2}}{{25}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{49}{625} \\\\  & =\\frac{625}{625}-\\frac{49}{625} \\\\  & =\\frac{576}{625}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{576}{625}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{24}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x$ lies in the fourth quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{24}{25}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{5})(\\frac{24}{25})-(-\\frac{4}{5})(-\\frac{7}{25}) \\\\  & =\\frac{72}{125}-\\frac{28}{125} \\\\  & =\\frac{44}{125}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315813 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3155a85f-7bd8-584c-a5ae-e2e18dd60cd2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Sine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the First Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "099a2c1f-beaa-5afe-9541-e1a795a182ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3-4\\sqrt{3}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315815} {:answer/id #uuid "f4cfd1c4-95e0-5f2d-a390-1e38f410ea42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3+4\\sqrt{3}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315816}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "099a2c1f-beaa-5afe-9541-e1a795a182ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3-4\\sqrt{3}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315815} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe923d70-f83e-5da0-86c0-493da2980ab9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315814}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "edc49973-dad3-556a-bdcd-3bd520d235fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-274503" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{3}{5},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=-\\frac{4}{5},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIf $v$ lies in the first quadrant, then $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{4}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{5})(\\frac{1}{2})+(-\\frac{4}{5})(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}-\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-4\\sqrt{3}}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315817 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "99d7256f-6130-52b4-8188-3b1388c63f2b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Sine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the Second, Third, or Fourth Quadrants"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e6c5cef-c6fa-570f-b11f-e9db297957c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-4}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315819} {:answer/id #uuid "bc4d443a-09bc-5762-adf1-0e80ecc15bcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}+4}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315820}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e6c5cef-c6fa-570f-b11f-e9db297957c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-4}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315819} :answer-field/id #uuid "53404b72-0590-5d6e-8b10-88b877435409" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1e0565cc-fe1d-5a62-baf7-f798e5ed2aca" :question/math-academy-id "q-274544" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nIf $u$ lies in the fourth quadrant, then $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡u=1-{cos}^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡u & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{9}-\\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the fourth quadrant, where sine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-(\\frac{1}{3})(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{6}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-4}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315821 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3155a85f-7bd8-584c-a5ae-e2e18dd60cd2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Sine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the First Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f0e3ac4-d0fa-51e7-8bbd-a166c35eec8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{85}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315823} {:answer/id #uuid "068e67d4-8e04-52bd-80ba-1a11dac0889c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{13}{85}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315824}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "068e67d4-8e04-52bd-80ba-1a11dac0889c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{13}{85}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315824} :answer-field/id #uuid "16235c5d-ebcd-58fa-aa8b-62f50eeeced9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315822}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "65dbc6eb-58dc-52ab-8f4d-4305875ea5bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-326363" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{8}{17},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{15}{17},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nIf $v$ lies in the first quadrant, then $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡v=1-{sin}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡v & =1-{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{9}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{8}{17})(\\frac{4}{5})-(\\frac{15}{17})(\\frac{3}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{85}-\\frac{45}{85} \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{85}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315825 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "99d7256f-6130-52b4-8188-3b1388c63f2b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Sine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the Second, Third, or Fourth Quadrants"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91d8d642-ca1c-5a55-a70f-d57269d5da40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315827}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91d8d642-ca1c-5a55-a70f-d57269d5da40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315827} :answer-field/id #uuid "00ccd5de-ce6b-5c85-93e4-c72b75e539f0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315826} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c4c582f-6eba-5297-a339-e8c04ac1288f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315829}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c4c582f-6eba-5297-a339-e8c04ac1288f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315829} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0909fec-c4d7-5df6-82ff-e8b8e0655cd8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3e422275-6cc3-5fb5-87ac-ee2442f2e695" :question/math-academy-id "q-326378" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{7}{25},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{24}{25},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the second quadrant, then\n\n| $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)$ | $=$ | {{field-1}}$\\,\\,-\\,$ {{field-2}}$\\sqrt{3}$ | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $50$ |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{4}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the second quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{7}{25})(-\\frac{1}{2})-(\\frac{24}{25})(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{50}-\\frac{24\\sqrt{3}}{50} \\\\  & =\\frac{-7-24\\sqrt{3}}{50}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184768 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "99d7256f-6130-52b4-8188-3b1388c63f2b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Sine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the Second, Third, or Fourth Quadrants"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a813978b-8f19-5e5d-abd2-a93aef026126" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{33}{65}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183498} {:answer/id #uuid "f2ae1ea7-3a94-53d0-9b09-98c9caefdad1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{49}{65}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183499} {:answer/id #uuid "35d271db-2267-5e22-af58-01253fbc426e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{63}{65}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183500} {:answer/id #uuid "496dfba6-4cb0-5d57-b8dc-d7cdcbc3cb76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{65}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183501} {:answer/id #uuid "73aa38c8-822b-509f-8d20-efed18896dec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{23}{65}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35d271db-2267-5e22-af58-01253fbc426e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{63}{65}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183500} :answer-field/id #uuid "f590cb36-f031-5022-9995-4ed0999d016d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183497}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4aa47f46-244d-51e1-8773-d950dc59e732" :question/math-academy-id "q-65423" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{4}{5},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡y=\\frac{12}{13},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=\\frac{5}{13}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $x$ lies in the second quadrant, find the value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡x=1-{cos}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡x & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1-{(-\\frac{4}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(-4)^{2}}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{25}-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x$ lies in the second quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =(\\frac{12}{13})(-\\frac{4}{5})-(\\frac{5}{13})(\\frac{3}{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{48}{65}-\\frac{15}{65} \\\\  & =-\\frac{63}{65}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
